You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: salesforce__team_performance
What the agent's work assumed: 挑收入最高的成员时，用另一份 revenue 汇总里的数就行
What was actually required or true: 按成员的成交总额排名

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "NULL revenue growth is replaced by zero before health scoring, awarding ten growth points instead of the specified else bucket of five.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "when coalesce(revenue_growth_qoq_percentage,0)>=-10 then 10 else 5",
      "source": "Revenue growth: 20 if > 10%, else 15 if > 0%, else 10 if ≥ -10%, else 5",
      "reason": "Teams without prior-quarter revenue receive a five-point score uplift that can change rankings."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Top revenue performer is ranked by the revenue-analytics aggregate rather than the explicitly required total won amount from user performance.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "arg_max(full_name,total_revenue_won)",
      "source": "top_revenue_performer = the full_name of the member with the highest total won amount",
      "reason": "Closed-only revenue and user-performance won amounts can differ for inconsistent opportunity flags."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The top performer fields return NULL when every ranking value is NULL rather than selecting an arbitrary member ranked last.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "arg_max(full_name,total_revenue_won) filter(where total_revenue_won is not null)",
      "source": "members without a value are ranked last; ties are arbitrary",
      "reason": "A team with no qualifying revenue still has members, but the filter removes all name candidates."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Team revenue and average revenue per member use closed-only revenue_data rather than user-performance total_won_amount, a source choice that needs confirmation.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "sum(total_revenue_won) as team_total_revenue; round(avg(total_revenue_won)) as avg_revenue_per_member",
      "source": "team_total_revenue ... sums of revenue ... avg_revenue_per_member = per-user average revenue won",
      "reason": "Users without closed revenue are excluded from the average instead of contributing their defaulted user-performance amount."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Quarterly revenue aggregates return NULL for owners without deals in that quarter, which may incorrectly propagate missingness into team growth.",
      "about": "data",
      "evidence_in_work": "sum(won_revenue) filter(where is_current_quarter); sum(won_revenue) filter(where is_previous_quarter)",
      "source": "current-quarter won revenue (sum where in current quarter), previous-quarter won revenue (sum where in previous quarter)",
      "reason": "A missing-quarter total and an actual zero have different consequences for growth and team reporting."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Negative nonzero previous-quarter revenue is excluded from growth calculation although the specification only excludes zero or NULL denominators.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "case when team_revenue_previous_quarter>0 then round(...) end",
      "source": "null if previous-quarter revenue is zero or null",
      "reason": "Staging normally nullifies negative opportunity amounts, so the mismatch is unlikely to affect this dataset."
    }
  }
]
